00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.680
There’s a lot of green in the world,&nbsp;
and that is thanks in no small part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:29.680
to the microcosmos. Not only did cyanobacteria&nbsp;
lay the foundation for an oxygenated Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.040
they laid the foundation for chloroplasts when&nbsp;
they were endosymbiotically consumed helping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.360
their host take on their photosynthetic machinery.
For most of us, the green we are confronted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:48.560
every day is a more macroscopic kind, the leaves&nbsp;
you see on trees and grass. And if you’re on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.080
hike, you might notice some squishy bundles of&nbsp;
green flush against a tree, or low to the ground.&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:03.840
“Oh, a moss,” you might tell yourself. Or “oh, a&nbsp;
lichen!” And then, perhaps, you move on, unsure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.960
of whether you’ve called it the right thing but&nbsp;
distracted by the many other sights around you.&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.280
But today we’re not going to let&nbsp;
that happen. We’re going to linger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.360
on our non-vascular plant friends&nbsp;
and look at them a little closer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.840
learning more about what they are, and why&nbsp;
only one of those things is actually a plant.&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.080
…it’s the moss. Moss is the plant.
More specifically, moss is a member of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:40.880
plant division called Bryophyta. And when we say&nbsp;
that moss is a non-vascular plant, what we mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.240
is that it doesn’t have the vascular tissue like a&nbsp;
xylem and phloem to transport water and nutrients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.880
around the plant. This lack of vasculature keeps&nbsp;
mosses short so that they can still transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:02.000
nutrients and water easily. And while they don’t&nbsp;
technically have roots or leaves as defined for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.280
vascular plants, you’ll see the equivalent&nbsp;
structures like their root-like filaments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:15.440
called rhizoids and also these leafy parts.
And mosses provide a lot of benefits to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.400
environment around them. They help prevent&nbsp;
erosion, and they can provide insulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.400
And we’ve seen ourselves how mosses provide a&nbsp;
home to some of our favorite microbial companions.&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.680
Lichen on the other hand though they are&nbsp;
sometimes called non-vascular plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:39.360
they are very definitely not&nbsp;
plants. So what is lichen?&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.720
In a 2010 Discovery article, the lichenologist&nbsp;
Trevor Goward described lichen as “fungi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.560
that have discovered agriculture.”
To be more specific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:01.600
lichen is a symbiotic union between a fungus and&nbsp;
an algae. The fungus provides access to nutrients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:07.280
as well as protection from the elements&nbsp;
and UV light. And in exchange, the algae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:13.360
provides its own photosynthetic capabilities.
Now that sounds straightforward enough, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.240
when you consider all the symbiotic relationships&nbsp;
we’ve learned about in the microcosmos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.760
Put together the right fungus, and the&nbsp;
right algae, and you get a lichen…right?&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.920
Well, scientists have attempted to&nbsp;
create lichens in the lab by culturing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.840
the appropriate partners together, but&nbsp;
with little success. And it turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.960
that that might be because we’ve underestimated&nbsp;
the number of partners involved. In one study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.760
scientists found that there was at least&nbsp;
one other fungal partner in their lichens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.200
And it could be that other species have many&nbsp;
many other members involved, making them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.440
far more complex than we have understood thus far.
For something so strange and complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:06.320
lichens are amazingly prevalent: they’re estimated&nbsp;
to cover up to around 6% of our planet’s surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.640
whether that’s on the side of a tree or on a rock&nbsp;
or even on dunes. And lichens have this ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.880
to not only latch on to all sorts of surfaces,&nbsp;
but to disrupt them and convert them into a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:25.200
habitable environment by releasing minerals and&nbsp;
creating the early ingredients for future soil.&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.600
And so because lichens are so important to&nbsp;
cultivating the seemingly inhospitable parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:38.800
our world, many have thought lichens were possibly&nbsp;
one of the first organisms to make it onto land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:45.600
But research from 2019 has suggested that lichens&nbsp;
didn’t find their way to land until well after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.960
vascular plants did. In fact, it could be as long&nbsp;
as 100 million years after, raising new questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:59.520
about how plants and our own earth evolved.
Now mosses are also part of this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.280
as they were likely among the earliest plants to&nbsp;
make it onto land. And their ancient spread may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.920
have played an important role in raising&nbsp;
oxygen levels to where they are today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.600
allowing for the evolution of not just other plant&nbsp;
life, but of more complex animal bodies as well.&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.040
With that said, the trick with evolution&nbsp;
is to know that creating a whole world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:26.400
is much more complicated than any one&nbsp;
step would have you believe. Mosses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:34.160
lichens carry their own worlds in their spreading&nbsp;
masses, not fully invisible like the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.640
but often underfoot and easy to ignore. But&nbsp;
maybe if you spot some on a walk, take a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:51.840
to look a little closer, and to wonder what&nbsp;
other secrets they might possibly hide.&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.720
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

